WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.980
Threading the carriage for plating
can be a bit tricky on this machine.

2
00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:10.980
Just remember that the point is to have
two yarns in the number one yarn feeder

3
00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:15.340
positioned so they remain
separate as you're knitting.

4
00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:18.020
Looking very closely at the yarn feeder,

5
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.460
there are two openings
that will be used for the plating.

6
00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:24.220
The goal is to have one yarn

7
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:30.400
in the regular opening and the contrasting
yarn in that back slot.

8
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.340
The easiest way I found to thread

9
00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:38.980
that back opening is to use a double-
eye needle or a darning needle.

10
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.680
Thread the needle with your yarn
and slip it down through that back slot.

11
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.820
The manual suggests

12
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:53.900
pushing a latch tool up from underneath
the carriage and using the hook to catch

13
00:00:53.930 --> 00:01:00.360
the yarn, and then pull the yarn
down to thread it in the feeder.

14
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.770
Using a work hook from a ribber works too.

15
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.800
Whichever method you use, make sure that
the two yarns are in separate feeders.

16
00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:14.050
They move smoothly
and they're not hung up anywhere.

17
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.260
Now, with the yarns positioned this way,
I can knit my plating.

18
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:20.720
One yarn will be positioned
in front of the other.

